# ATLAS AI AGENT ARCHITECTURE & CODING LAWS

You are a senior engineer building "Atlas AI", a production-grade Telegram Assistant in Node.js, TypeScript, grammY, and Prisma (PostgreSQL).

## LAWS OF ARCHITECTURE & EXECUTION

### LAW 1: Strict Modular Separation
Never place all logic into a single file or monolithic handler. Enforce this directory layout:
src/
├── bot/            # grammY bot setup, command handlers, inline keyboards, middleware
├── services/       # External APIs, database client, calendar integration, web search
├── ai/             # System prompts, Zod output schemas, LLM orchestration
├── db/             # Prisma client, database repositories/queries
├── utils/          # Telegram HTML helpers, text splitters, error boundaries
└── config/         # Environment variable validation with Zod

### LAW 2: Telegram HTML Formatting Only (NO RAW MARKDOWN)
- NEVER output raw Markdown like `**bold**` or `_italic_` in string responses. Telegram parsing fails easily on raw markdown.
- ALWAYS use Telegram-supported HTML tags ONLY: `<b>bold</b>`, `<i>italic</i>`, `<code>code</code>`, `<a href="...">link</a>`.
- Escape raw `<` and `>` characters in variable text to prevent malformed HTML parsing exceptions.

### LAW 3: Native UX & Defensive Delivery
- ALWAYS trigger `await ctx.replyWithChatAction('typing')` BEFORE executing any asynchronous service, DB query, or LLM call.
- All message delivery MUST route through a utility function `sendSafeTelegramMessage()` that:
  1. Catches Telegram HTTP 429 (rate-limit) errors and waits before retrying.
  2. Automatically splits messages exceeding 4,000 characters into safe chunks.
  3. Validates and falls back to plain text if HTML parsing fails.

### LAW 4: Decoupled Handler Logic
Bot command handlers (`bot.command()`, `bot.on()`) must remain extremely thin. Handlers should ONLY:
1. Extract user payload from `ctx`.
2. Call a service layer function (e.g., `UserService.getOrCreateUser()`).
3. Pass results to a view/formatter layer and render via `ctx.reply()`.
Zero AI processing or complex database queries are allowed inside the event listener callbacks directly.

### LAW 5: Schema-Driven LLM Outputs
Every LLM call must return structured JSON validated via Zod. Text generation without Zod validation is strictly forbidden. Include defensive try-catch fallbacks to parse partial or malformed LLM responses gracefully.

### LAW 6: External API Resilience
When integrating `yahoo-finance2` or any external data provider, ALWAYS wrap the network call in a `try/catch` block. If the financial API fails, gracefully return a fallback message to the user rather than crashing the Node process.

### LAW 7: Vercel AI SDK for Structured Output
For LLM interactions, strictly use the `@ai-sdk/openai` (or anthropic) provider with the `generateObject` method. Do not write manual regex parsers for JSON. Pass the Zod schema directly to `generateObject` to guarantee type safety.

### LAW 8: Strict UI Separation
The LLM should output RAW JSON data (Headline, Facts, Sentiment). It should NEVER output Telegram HTML directly. Create a separate formatter utility function (`src/utils/formatters.ts`) that takes the validated JSON object and wraps it in the correct `<b>`, `<i>`, and `<code>` HTML tags.

### LAW 9: Cron Job Safety & Non-Blocking Background Tasks
- All scheduled background jobs (`node-cron`) MUST run inside a defensive try/catch block. A failed cron execution must NEVER crash the primary Telegram bot process.
- Avoid duplicate deliveries: Track the last briefing delivery date/time per user or log execution status cleanly.

### LAW 10: Strict Context Windowing (Memory Limits)
- When fetching chat history from the database to send to Gemini, ALWAYS limit the query to the last 6 messages max (`take: 6`, ordered by `createdAt: desc`, then inverted to chronological order).
- Never pass the entire raw chat history to the LLM; keep context lightweight for speed and low latency.

### LAW 11: Demo-Friendly Trigger Overrides
- EVERY proactive or scheduled feature must have a manual command fallback (e.g., `/briefing` triggers an instant briefing without waiting for the scheduled time). This is mandatory for manual testing and video demos.

### LAW 12: Calendar & Meeting Context Synthesis
- When generating a Daily Briefing or answering queries, cross-reference upcoming calendar events with live financial data. (e.g., If the user has a meeting with "Nvidia Partners", explicitly highlight $NVDA market news in their briefing).
- ALWAYS maintain a clean fallback: If Google Calendar OAuth tokens are missing or invalid, serve mock agenda items seamlessly so the bot never throws a 500 error during a live evaluator test.

### LAW 13: Cloud Deployment & Health Checks
- Ensure the bot runs smoothly in a cloud environment (e.g., Render, Railway, or Fly.io).
- Add a lightweight HTTP server (`express` or native `http`) on `PORT` so cloud hosting platforms pass health checks and keep the process alive.

### LAW 14: Zero-Error User Experience
- Catch all unhandled exceptions at the process level (`process.on('unhandledRejection')`).
- If an unhandled error occurs during a Telegram message, log it silently to console and send a polite error message to the user instead of letting the bot freeze.